\section{Cross-Model Type Ranking Concordance}
\label{app:kendall_w}

To quantify agreement among models on the relative difficulty of ambiguity types, we compute Kendall's coefficient of concordance $W$. For the five main-study models across all six types, $W = 0.398$ ($\chi^2(5) = 9.94$, $p = 0.072$), indicating moderate but non-significant concordance. Including the two replication models (Gemini 2.5 Flash and LLaMA 4 Scout) over five shared types (excluding coreferential, which was not included in replication), concordance reaches significance: $W = 0.353$ ($\chi^2(4) = 9.89$, $p = 0.038$). In both analyses, incompleteness consistently ranks highest (mean rank 5.5 / 4.5), while coreferential (2.0, five-model only) and conditional precedence (2.8 / 2.1) rank lowest, supporting the graded risk structure identified in \S\ref{sec:results}.

\section{C4 Interaction Without Authorization Scope}
\label{app:c4_sans_auth}

Excluding authorization scope, the residual model$\times$type interaction vanishes ($\chi^2(16) = 7.13$, $p = 0.97$; logistic regression LR test: $\chi^2(16) = 9.22$, $p = 0.90$), confirming that the C4 interaction is driven entirely by authorization scope---the type most plausibly targeted by differential safety training.

\section{Core Analysis Excluding Authorization Scope}
\label{app:sans_auth}

To assess the robustness of core findings to authorization scope's measurement instability (\S\ref{sec:results}), we recompute primary analyses excluding all authorization scope episodes ($n = 2{,}497$ remaining).

C1 remains robust: pooled $\Delta = +33.6$\,pp (vs.\ $+34.0$\,pp overall). Variance decomposition is stable: clause identity 23.6\% (vs.\ 22.4\%), type 0.4\% (unchanged), model 0.9\%, condition 15.5\%, residual 60.0\%. The type--mechanism association weakens slightly at the 5-type level (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.30$, NMI $= 0.13$) but remains highly significant ($p < 10^{-28}$). These results confirm that authorization scope's outlier behavior does not drive the primary conclusions.

